  <!-- Caravan guards carry a bow (#591).

       A flying player can strip a dromad caravan at no risk, and the cause is narrower than the
       forum version of the claim. Caravans are guarded - 2-4 Caravan Guards at level 30 with 165 HP
       - but both equipment builders in the game draw only from "Melee Weapons N", "Armor N" and
       "Junk N". There is no missile weapon at any tier, so a guard cannot be armed against a flyer
       under any roll.

       What a player actually sees is not a rout. Kill.TakeAction's flee branch sits inside
       `if (num == 1)`, so it only fires when the flyer is adjacent; the ranged branch above it has
       no flight check anywhere and Brain.MaxMissileRange is 80, the whole zone. So the guard walks
       under the flyer, becomes adjacent, runs for two turns - Flee(Target, 2) is a duration, not a
       distance - and comes back. A ping-pong. Give it something that reaches and the ranged branch
       handles the rest.

       Compound Bow rather than Short Bow, and this is the part that is not obvious. An arrow's
       StrengthPenetration is a *cap*, not a bonus: MissileWeapon builds `num3 = BasePenetration`
       and `num4 = BasePenetration + StrengthPenetration`, adds the wielder's stat modifier to num3
       ONLY when the weapon declares ProjectilePenetrationStat, and Stat.RollDamagePenetrations then
       uses Math.Min(num3, num4). BaseArrowProjectile sets BasePenetration="0". Short Bow declares no
       ProjectilePenetrationStat, so a Short Bow fires every arrow in the game at penetration 0 -
       Wooden and Zetachrome alike. Compound Bow declares Strength, and it is the only other bow.

       Steel Arrow because the cap should meet the modifier and not exceed it. Caravan Guard inherits
       Strength sValue="14,1d3,(t)d1" from BaseHumanoid and (t) resolves as Level / 5 + 1 = 7 at
       level 30, so Strength is 22-24 and GetScoreModifier - floor((score - 16) * 0.5) - gives 3 or
       4. Steel caps at 3. Wooden would waste the modifier at 2; Carbide would let it run to 4.

       Merge appends rather than replaces. <inventoryobject> is an Unnamed node in
       ObjectBlueprintLoader, and Unnamed merging is `Unnamed.AddRange(other.Unnamed)` - so anything
       Freehold later gives this guard survives alongside these two rows. Charter rule 1.

       The guards, not the trader. The trader keeps its AISelfPreservation Threshold="40" and stays
       out of the fight, which is a different design and not this one.

       Not retroactive. DromadCaravan.Created persists, so a caravan already materialised in a save
       keeps its unarmed guards. No migration is needed and none is possible. -->

  <object Name="Caravan Guard" Load="Merge">
    <inventoryobject Blueprint="Compound Bow" Number="1" />
    <inventoryobject Blueprint="Steel Arrow" Number="15-25" />
  </object>

  <!-- Nima Ruda keeps dunelace on the shelf, rather than on a timer (#858).

       She is the apothecary the dish was described against - hand-placed in Joppa.rpm at cell 45,2
       beside the Alchemist Table, and the first apothecary most players ever meet - and the ten
       population merges reach her only through `Village Apothecary 0`, her restocker's table. That
       is enough in principle and not in practice, because `GenericInventoryRestocker` rolls the
       table once on the first StartTradeEvent, when LastRestockTick is still 0, and after that only
       every 6000 turns with the player in her zone at a Chance-scaled roll. On a save that has
       already traded with her, dunelace could not appear for five in-world days - and then on half
       of those rolls, because Chance is 50.

       "The shop route carries availability" does not hold if the reference shop is a coin flip five
       days out. So she carries some outright, and the table roll still applies on top.

       Merge appends rather than replaces: <inventoryobject> is an Unnamed node in
       ObjectBlueprintLoader and Unnamed merging is `Unnamed.AddRange(other.Unnamed)`, so everything
       Freehold gives her survives alongside this. Charter rule 1.

       Not retroactive, and nothing can make it so - a Nima already built into a save keeps the
       inventory she was built with. A new character has it from the first trade. -->
  <object Name="Nima Ruda" Load="Merge">
    <inventoryobject Blueprint="Vixy_Dried Dunelace" Number="1-2" />
  </object>

